#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c8f0fa1-a86a-577f-bdc9-fc7427faa18d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Image of a Line Given by an Equation in Slope-Intercept Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fd19ea-c633-517a-b1c9-ee6f8212d9fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13x+3y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290321} {:answer/id #uuid "613759be-094a-5654-974d-97ea77254189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+3y+12=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290322} {:answer/id #uuid "b842027f-3e17-54b4-a2d1-ffe6e2b3cf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+13y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290323} {:answer/id #uuid "73f7fd2a-8954-5b8d-92f4-2fbd1546f8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13x+8y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290324} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e4427b-7761-5a55-b634-a5cadfd2e927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+3y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b842027f-3e17-54b4-a2d1-ffe6e2b3cf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+13y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290323} :answer-field/id #uuid "625f4560-f4c4-52df-8e53-891c0a200f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a72a83e-c00c-5e25-8dc9-1f34b89688c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-77776" :question/problem "Consider the line $y=4x-1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $y=4x-1$ passes through the points $Q(0,-1)$ and $R(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $Q$ and $R$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${Q}^{′}(3,-2)$ and ${R}^{′}(-10,6).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${Q}^{′}$ and ${R}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6-(-2)}{-10-3}(x-3) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{8}{13}(x-3) \\\\ 13y+26 & =-8x+24 \\\\ 8x+13y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
